## Supabase (dev database)

A **playground/dev Supabase** is wired up for you (a scratch DB — **not**
production). A ready-to-use project lives in **`/workspace/supabase/`**: schema
migrations, an example RLS table, helper scripts, and a full guide in
`/workspace/supabase/README.md` — **read that before using Supabase**.

Quick reference:
- Browser apps: prefer **same-origin** `createClient(location.origin + '{{ playground_supabase_same_origin_path | default('/api') }}', ANON_KEY)` — no CORS (Caddy proxies `{{ playground_supabase_same_origin_path | default('/api') }}/*` on each pgN to Supabase). Cross-origin `$SUPABASE_URL` also works.
- `$SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` and `$SUPABASE_DB_URL` **bypass RLS — server/CLI only, NEVER in a frontend bundle**.
- Migrate: `cd /workspace/supabase && ./scripts/db-migrate` · shell: `./scripts/db-psql` · smoke test: `./scripts/check`.
- **This instance is SHARED** across all envs/apps. Namespace every object by **app
  name**, never by environment/pgN — the same app may deploy to pg2, pg3, or later
  prod, so `pg3_*` names would bind data to a slot. Prefix tables/policies/buckets/
  function-slugs like `guestbook_*`. REST exposes **only the `public` schema**
  (`PGRST106` for anything else), so a custom per-app schema works only server-side
  (`service_role`/`SUPABASE_DB_URL`), not on the browser/anon path — there, prefixing
  in `public` is the only isolation. Two deployments of the same app against this one
  instance intentionally share data; real isolation is a separate-instance concern.
- **Teardown/reset must be prefix-scoped too — the prefix rule governs deletion, not
  just creation.** An app's reset script drops ONLY its own objects: enumerate
  `pg_tables`/`pg_views`/`pg_proc`/`pg_policies` filtered by the app prefix
  (`bestand\_%`, `ext\_%`) and drop those; delete only the app's storage bucket +
  prefixed `storage.objects` policies. **Never `drop schema public cascade`, never a
  blanket drop, never touch another app's objects** — the instance is shared, so an
  unscoped teardown is data loss for every other app. (`/workspace/pg4/db/reset.sql`
  is the reference shape.)
- **Platform capabilities & limits (self-hosted — verified live):**
  - **Extensions:** `pg_trgm`, `btree_gist`, `pgtap`, `pgcrypto` are **enable-on-demand**
    (`create extension if not exists …` in a migration works). **`pg_cron` IS
    available** — provisioning pre-creates the extension, so `cron.schedule(...)`
    works via `SUPABASE_DB_URL`/migrations (server-side as `postgres`; the `cron`
    schema is not reachable through REST/anon). Cron jobs are **instance-wide** —
    name them with your app prefix (`myapp_cleanup`), and prefix-scope teardown
    (`cron.unschedule` only your own jobs), like every other shared object.
  - **Edge Functions: deploy = drop files, NOT `supabase functions deploy`** (that
    CLI command only targets the hosted cloud). Your deploy path:
    `/workspace/supabase/functions/<name>/index.ts` (see `functions/hello/` for the
    shape) → the runtime resolves `/functions/v1/<name>` from this dir. **New**
    functions are picked up immediately (worker per request); after **editing** an
    existing one run `playgroundctl functions reload` (sheds cached workers —
    otherwise stale for up to ~60 s). Name functions with your app prefix
    (`myapp_cleanup`) like every other shared object. The mount is read-only for
    the runtime; calls need a valid JWT (anon key) like the other API paths.
    For simple authorized DB logic, a **`SECURITY DEFINER` Postgres RPC** is still
    often the leaner tool — reach for a function when you need Deno/TS, fetch to
    external APIs, or secrets that must not live in the DB.
  - **`storage.objects` blocks direct `DELETE`** (trigger `storage.protect_delete`,
    "Use the Storage API instead"). Prefix-scoped teardown/reset scripts must wrap
    storage deletes in a tolerant block or they abort.
  - **A Postgres VIEW over an RLS table needs `with (security_invoker = true)`** —
    otherwise it runs with the (superuser) owner's rights and **bypasses RLS**,
    leaking all rows through the view.

