## What you can do

Read-only against the host Docker daemon (never write):

```bash
playgroundctl host ps
playgroundctl host image ls
playgroundctl host compose ls
```

Read-only **host diagnostics** — when apps feel slow, attribute the load instead
of guessing from `loadavg` alone:

```bash
playgroundctl host stats            # per-container CPU/mem/net/block snapshot + docker system df -v
playgroundctl host diag             # CPU vs I/O vs storage: PSI, vmstat, iostat, mpstat, df -h/-i, free
playgroundctl host promql '<expr>'  # read-only Prometheus instant query (trends/history)
```

- `host stats` answers "**who**, right now" (which container eats the CPUs/disk).
- `host diag` answers "**which resource**" — its output ends with interpretation
  rules (PSI/iowait/%util thresholds).
- `host promql` answers "**since when / trend**". Prometheus scrapes node-exporter
  (host) **and cAdvisor (per-container)**. Useful expressions:
  - `topk(5, sum by (name) (rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{name!=""}[5m])))` — top CPU eaters
  - `container_memory_working_set_bytes{name!=""}` — per-container RAM
  - `node_load1 / on() group_left() count(count(node_cpu_seconds_total) by (cpu))` — load per core (the `on() group_left()` is required — plain division silently returns empty due to label matching)
  - `rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="iowait"}[5m])` — iowait trend
  - PromQL subqueries give history: `avg_over_time(node_load1[1h:])`
  - Note: cAdvisor covers the **host** daemon's containers (the heavy backing
    services). The rootless pgN containers are not in cAdvisor — they are idle
    static servers and never the bottleneck; use `playground ps`/`compose logs`
    for them.

Rootless **playground** daemon — daemon-wide (no `--env`):

```bash
playgroundctl playground envs
playgroundctl playground ps
playgroundctl playground image ls
playgroundctl playground image rm playground/<image>:<tag>
playgroundctl playground compose ls
```

Rootless **playground** daemon — environment-scoped (add `--env <name>`; the
default env is used if omitted):

```bash
playgroundctl playground compose up      --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}
playgroundctl playground compose ps      --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}
playgroundctl playground compose logs 100 --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}
playgroundctl playground compose stop    --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}
playgroundctl playground compose restart --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}
playgroundctl playground compose down    --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}
playgroundctl playground compose destroy --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}   # down + volumes
playgroundctl playground compose build   --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}
playgroundctl playground compose reset   --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}   # destroy + up
playgroundctl playground compose kill    --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}

playgroundctl playground http get /      --env {{ playground_environments[0] }}   # fetch that env's app
```

`http get` is how you VERIFY a deploy: your container has no network path to the
app (it publishes on the host loopback), but the broker does. It fetches the
**selected env's** port and returns the HTTP status line + first 8 KB of body —
check that your change is actually visible.

## Rules

- You **cannot** run write/compose actions on `host` — they are rejected.
- `--env` is only valid for the environment-scoped commands above. `env` names
  are fixed by the host (`playgroundctl playground envs`); you cannot create new
  ones from here.
- `playgroundctl playground image rm` only accepts images whose name starts with
  `playground/` (e.g. `playground/app:latest`). Tag images you build under that
  prefix if you want to be able to remove them.
- Never expect access to `/var/run/docker.sock`. It does not exist in this
  container by design.

